t * f‖₂ ≤ ‖G_t‖₁ · ‖f‖₂ = 1 · ‖f‖₂ = ‖f‖₂.\n\nBut actually, the L² contraction for the heat kernel follows more directly: since G_t ≥ 0 and ∫G_t = 1, by Jensen's inequality (or Minkowski's integral inequality):\n‖∫ G_t(x-y) f(y) dy‖₂ ≤ ∫ G_t(x-y) ‖f(y)‖₂ dy = ‖f‖₂ · ∫ G_t = ‖f‖₂.\n\nWait, that's not quite right. The proper tool is **Minkowski's integral inequality**: \n‖G_t * f‖₂ = ‖∫ G_t(·-y) f(y) dy‖₂ ≤ ∫ |G_t(x-y)| · |f(y)| · ‖...‖₂ dx ...\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: use the **Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for convolutions** which gives the L² contraction directly when the kernel is in L¹:\n\n‖G * f‖₂² = ∫ |∫ G(x-y) f(y) dy|² dx ≤ ∫ (∫ |G(x-y)| |f(y)| dy)² dx\n\nBy Cauchy-Schwarz on the inner integral with ∫G = 1:\n(∫ G(x-y) |f(y)| dy)² ≤ (∫ G(x-y) dy)(∫ G(x-y) |f(y)|² dy) = ∫ G(x-y) |f(y)|² dy\n\nSo ‖G * f‖₂² ≤ ∫∫ G(x-y) |f(y)|² dy dx = ∫ |f(y)|² (∫ G(x-y) dx) dy = ∫ |f(y)|² dy = ‖f‖₂².\n\nThis uses Cauchy-Schwarz weighted by G (a probability measure). Let me formalize this approach.\n\nBut first, let me check if Mathlib has the general version of Minkowski's integral inequality:"},{"seq":9,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T05:57:37.746778712Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"minkowski\\|Minkowski\\|integral.*norm.*integral\\|eLpNorm.*integral\\|integral.*eLp"]},{"seq":10,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T05:57:48.407454541Z","text":"Mathlib has `norm_integral_le_integral_norm` (triangle inequality for integrals) but not the general Minkowski integral inequality for L^p norms.\n\nGiven the difficulty of proving the general Young's inequality, let me focus on what's actually needed for a96A04: a specific statement that the heat convolution contracts L². I'll prove a self-contained version using the Cauchy-Schwarz weighted approach.\n\nLet me write the proof and add it to the a96A04 file:"},{"seq":11,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T05:57:48.408179197Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A04/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":12,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T05:58:10.207984598Z","text":"Now let me try to prove part (b) — the L² contraction. The approach: use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality weighted by the heat kernel (which is nonneg and integrates to 1).\n\nFor `G_t * f (x) = ∫ G_t(x-y) f(y) dy`, the key inequality is:\n|∫ G_t(x-y) f(y) dy|² ≤ (∫ G_t(x-y) dy)(∫ G_t(x-y) |f(y)|² dy) = ∫ G_t(x-y) |f(y)|² dy\n\nThis is Cauchy-Schwarz with measure `G_t(x-y) dy` (a probability measure since ∫G_t = 1).\n\nThen: ∫|G_t*f|² dx ≤ ∫∫ G_t(x-y) |f(y)|² dy dx = ∫ |f(y)|² (∫ G_t(x-y) dx) dy = ∫ |f(y)|² dy = ‖f‖₂².\n\nThe swap of integrals (Fubini) and the inner equality `∫ G_t(x-y) dx = ∫ G_t(x) dx = 1` (by translation invariance of Lebesgue measure) are the key steps.\n\nLet me write the proof:"},{"seq":13,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T05:58:10.208328146Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A04/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":14,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T05:58:10.208384056Z","text":"[edit_file ✗ old-string not found in /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A04/lean/Main.lean]"},{"seq":15,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T05:58:19.049919086Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -n \"L² contraction\\|heatConv_L2_le\\|Young\" problems/a96A04/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":16,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T05:58:45.903138546Z","text":"Let me try a different approach. Instead of the full proof, let me prove the **key lemma** that would make this work — the L² contraction via Minkowski's integral inequality. Let me first check if Mathlib has `MeasureTheory.norm_integral_le_integral_norm` which is the L¹ version, and see if there's a generalization to Lᵖ:\n\nActually, let me try a completely different angle. Mathlib might have `MeasureTheory.MemLp.integral_mul` or similar for bounding integrals of Lᵖ functions:\n\nLet me focus on what I CAN prove. The task says \"prove